{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 2, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. After the death of his father Raymond III succeeded him as count of Tripoli.\n\n*Early Life and Reign*\nRaymond III was born in 1140. His father Raymond II was assassinated in 1152 and Raymond III succeeded him as count of Tripoli. At the time of his succession Raymond III was only 12 years old and the regency was held by his mother Hodierna. In 1155 Raymond III came of age and took control of the county.\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a close ally of King Amalric I and supported him in his military campaigns. In 1164 Raymond III led a successful campaign against the Fatimid Caliphate and captured the city of Banias. He also participated in the siege of Alexandria in 1167.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin*\nIn 1187 Raymond III was defeated by Saladin at the Battle of Hattin. The battle was a significant defeat for the Crusader states and led to the loss of Jerusalem and other territories. Raymond III was captured by Saladin and released after paying a ransom.\n\n*Later Life and Death*\nAfter his release Raymond III returned to Tripoli and continued to rule the county. He died in 1187 and was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV. Raymond III was married to Eschiva of Bures and had no children. His legacy is remembered as a skilled military leader and a key figure in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem.\n\nRaymond III also played a significant role in the development of the county of Tripoli. He established trade relations with the Italian city-states and encouraged the growth of the city of Tripoli. He also built several castles and fortifications to protect the county from Muslim attacks.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0041340